        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.327,
            0.78,
            0.376
          ],
          "content": "Is it possible to draw $\\overrightarrow { \\mathrm { O C } }$ such that the two angles are equal to each other in size?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.803,
            0.313,
            0.891,
            0.38
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_15_image_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.388,
            0.321,
            0.409
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Let’s Explore"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.415,
            0.884,
            0.46
          ],
          "content": "We can try to solve this problem using a piece of paper. Recall that when a fold is made, it creates a crease which is straight."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.462,
            0.886,
            0.53
          ],
          "content": "Take a rectangular piece of paper and on one of its sides, mark the straight angle AOB. By folding, try to get a line (crease) passing through O that divides ∠AOB into two equal angles."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.194,
            0.53,
            0.419,
            0.551
          ],
          "content": "How can it be done?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.817,
            0.884,
            0.863
          ],
          "content": "Fold the paper such that OB overlaps with OA. Observe the crease and the two angles formed."
        },
        {
